# What is the LIVE per-superblock quantizer-hint channel worth?
#
# Three questions, deliberately kept separate because a map can win one and
# lose another:
#   1. ACTIVATION STEP  - how far does merely switching delta-q on move the
#                         score, at an unchanged quantizer?
#   2. INTERPOLATION    - can a MIXED map place the score BETWEEN two
#                         adjacent achievable lattice points, finely AND
#                         monotonically enough for a search to aim with?
#   3. RD               - does the diffmap-derived map buy a better score at
#                         MATCHED BYTES?
#
# date          2026-08-06
# host          Darwin 26.5.2 (macOS), Apple aarch64, 12 cores, 24 GiB RAM
# rustc         rustc 1.97.1 (8bab26f4f 2026-07-14)
#
# ENCODER PROVENANCE (this is the whole point of this block -- the encoder
# was moving under this work and every cell here is from ONE build)
#   zenavif       b19bc3c2 "feat(two-shot): quantizer-space lattice
#                 targeting at a fixed 2-encode budget" + the harness commit
#                 on top of it
#   ravif         path dep ../ravif/ravif @ 619d81adcaa5dd5546d55bf669560e9eeb74d080
#                 branch feat/zenrav1e-master-bump, working tree CLEAN
#                 ravif/src content sha256
#                 ddf30aee574e292699636a687e4888f4ac38c4a696430eeb118602f07fef4a8c
#   zenrav1e      sibling master @ 4d1ed620703d7eb94bb24467b44c5b498896af62, clean
#                 src content sha256
#                 face801cf28fbafcbdf2f68f5d299f9d6699af363ddd2e2523ce7bd2ebc52d2b
#   harness bin   sha256 71ffc7915ef3ffc57d2d16b8dfc3a0087ff9e4f2b924cb5100a864d1f44ed3b1
#   zensim        git imazen/zensim main @ e4b875b5 (0.3.0), ZensimProfile::codec_target() == B
#   FRAME_HINTS_LIVE = TRUE (this is what made the measurement possible at all)
#   NOT built with -C target-cpu=native
#
#   Every cell ran on that single statically linked binary, so the five
#   release-gated speed-tier arms (S1_DEEP, S6_TX_SIZE_RDO, S6_PART_PRUNE,
#   S6_INTRA7, S10_RETIER) are OFF here -- they were flipped in ravif's
#   working tree at 08:10, after this binary was built and while this sweep
#   was already running. The sweep therefore does NOT span that change.
#   The structural conclusions (activation step exists and is large;
#   dithering is fine-grained but non-monotone) are properties of AV1's
#   per-SB delta-q coding, not of those arms.
#
# command
#   cargo build --release --features two-pass-zensim,auto-tune \
#       --example zensim_loop_bench
#   RAYON_NUM_THREADS=1 nice -n 5 ./target/release/examples/zensim_loop_bench \
#       hintprobe <6-source manifest> 6 64,256,1024 90,120,160 0.94,0.88
#
# analysis
#   python3 scripts/hyperparam/analyze_zensim_hintprobe.py \
#       <(zstd -dc benchmarks/zensim_hint_probe_2026-08-06.tsv.zst)
#   (deterministic; reproduces benchmarks/zensim_hint_probe_2026-08-06.summary.txt)
#
# grid          6 sources (4 clic2025 photos + gb82-sc imessage + codec_wiki)
#               x long edges {64, 256, 1024}
#               x base quantizers {90, 120, 160}  -- chosen to straddle the
#                 three delta_q resolution bands (res 2 / 4 / 8, keyed on the
#                 base quantizer by zenrav1e variance_boost_delta_q_res_log2)
#               = 54 cells, 1,578 encode+decode+score cells
#
# variants per (cell, base quantizer)
#   lattice         un-hinted encodes at quantizer base-2 ..= base+6. These
#                   are the achievable points any interpolation claim must be
#                   judged against. NOTE: in this run the `k` column is 0 for
#                   lattice rows; they are emitted in that fixed quantizer
#                   order, and the analyser recovers the quantizer from row
#                   position (later runs write it into `k` directly).
#   activated_base  delta-q switched on with every superblock's delta
#                   quantizing to zero (one entry at 0.999, below every
#                   delta_q resolution). Isolates the ACTIVATION step from
#                   any actual per-superblock movement.
#   dither          k of N superblocks scaled finer, k strided over 0..N,
#                   subset chosen by van der Corput ordering so it is
#                   scattered and nested in k rather than a contiguous stripe.
#   diffmap         the map two_pass_zensim::sb_q_scale_from_diffmap builds
#                   from an un-hinted encode at the same quantizer, at
#                   strengths {0.5, 1.0, 2.0}.
#
# THE TRAP THIS PROBE FELL INTO FIRST, recorded so nobody repeats it
#   AV1 codes per-superblock delta_q at a RESOLUTION of 1/2/4/8 quantizer
#   indices. The first version used a 1.5% scale (0.985), which is below that
#   resolution at ~76% of quantizer indices, so every superblock's delta
#   quantized to zero and the sweep came back IDENTICAL for every dithered
#   fraction. That reads exactly like "the encoder ignores the map's content"
#   and it is wrong: a `hintdiag` control with one / half / all superblocks
#   at scale 0.5 gives +536 / +2,991 / +5,750 bytes on the same cell. The
#   scales here (0.94, 0.88) are sized to survive the resolution.
#
#   Second trap: a map whose length does not match the superblock grid is
#   SILENTLY IGNORED, so a wrong grid is indistinguishable from a real null.
#   The `wrong_len` control in `hintdiag` pins this, and the harness asserts
#   the grid it builds. Verify bytes actually changed before believing any
#   null from this channel.
#
# columns       image size w h base_qi variant scale k sbs bytes zensim enc_ms
#               k       = superblocks dithered (0 for non-dither variants)
#               sbs     = ceil(w/64) * ceil(h/64), the map length the encoder
#                         requires
#               enc_ms  = encode only; NOT comparable across rows here, the
#                         box was shared. Use it for nothing.
#
# scheduling    nice -n 5, NOT nice -n 19. macOS maps high nice values onto
#               the background QoS class (efficiency cores only): ~40x
#               wall-clock difference measured 2026-08-06 on this box.
#
# STORAGE: committed zstd-compressed (146 KB -> 12.6 KB) per the repo's 30 KB
# rule. Read it with:
#
#     zstd -dc benchmarks/zensim_hint_probe_2026-08-06.tsv.zst | less
#
# The .summary.txt beside it carries the aggregates without decompressing.
